course: biology
lesson: 57
title: Roots, stems and leaves
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A plant is built like a house with plumbing: roots underground drink water and hold on tight, the stem is a bundle of pipes and a pillar, and the leaves are solar panels that make food.
explain: Try the celery trick: stand a celery stick in blue food colouring overnight, and the leaves turn blue at the edges, because water is pulled up thin tubes in the stem as it evaporates from the leaves, like a drink up a straw.
explain: Tie a plastic bag over a leafy branch on a sunny day and it fogs up inside: that water came up from the roots and escaped through tiny holes in the leaves called stomata.
explain: Plants change these parts for special jobs: a cactus turned its leaves into spines, a pea turned shoots into curly tendrils, an onion stores food in swollen leaves, and a nettle grows stinging hairs.
explain: Cartoons show plants drinking through their leaves when it rains and roots as just a few strings; in real life almost all the water goes in through the roots, which can spread far wider and deeper than the plant above.
fact: A dandelion has a thick deep taproot that stores food, so it grows back if you only pull off the leaves; grass has many thin fibrous roots near the surface; a carrot is a taproot too, growing down with no buds or leaves along it, while stems have buds.
fact: Roots are covered with tiny root hairs, each a single long cell, which give a much bigger surface to soak up water and minerals; one rye plant was measured with about 600 km of roots, not even counting the root hairs.
fact: Stems have thin tubes that carry water up from the roots, and other tubes that carry sugar round the plant.
fact: Leaf veins carry water in and sugar out, and hold the leaf flat like the ribs of an umbrella; leaves are thin and flat to catch light and let gases in and out.
fact: Leaves lose water vapour through tiny holes called stomata, mostly on the underside; this water loss, transpiration, pulls more water up from the roots.
fact: A cactus's spines are leaves changed into points; its fat green stem stores water and does the photosynthesis, and a thick waxy skin keeps water in; many cacti open their stomata only at night, when it is cool and much less water is lost.
fact: Pea plants climb with tendrils that wrap round whatever they touch, reaching the light without building a thick stem; ivy climbs with tiny gripping roots.
fact: Nettle hairs have glass-like tips that snap off and inject stinging chemicals; the sting stops grazing animals, so few animals eat nettles, and cooking destroys it.
fact: An onion is a bulb whose layers are swollen leaves storing food for next year's growth.
fact: In autumn trees stop making green chlorophyll, so hidden yellows and oranges show through, and some trees make new reds; they take back useful material before the leaves fall, because thin leaves would be hurt by frost and would lose water when the ground is frozen.
fact: The sensitive plant, Mimosa pudica, folds its leaves within seconds when touched, as water rushes out of cells at the base of each leaf; plants have no nerves or muscles.
fact: The Amazon giant water lily has floating leaves up to about 3 metres across, with air-filled ribs underneath and spines to protect it from fish; the ribs inspired the Crystal Palace in London.
exercise: What kind of root does a dandelion have? | A thick deep taproot | Many thin roots near the surface | No roots at all, only a stem | Roots that grow on top of the soil
exercise: What is the white fuzz on the roots of a cress seedling? | Tiny root hairs that soak up water | Mould growing on the roots in the damp | Seeds getting ready to fall | Spider silk from the soil
exercise: Why do root hairs help a plant? | They give a bigger surface for soaking up water and minerals | They keep the roots warm in winter, like a fluffy woolly blanket | They tickle worms out of the way | They make the root heavier so it sinks
exercise: Why do the celery leaves turn blue in coloured water? | Water is pulled up tubes in the stem into the leaves | The leaves soak up the blue colour from the air around them | The colour climbs the outside of the stick | Celery always turns blue at night
exercise: What do the veins you see in a leaf held up to the light do? | Carry water in and sugar out, and hold the leaf flat | Carry red blood around the plant, just like our veins do | Show how old the leaf is | Let rain soak into the plant
exercise: Why does a plastic bag tied over a leafy branch fog up inside? | Water vapour escapes from the leaves through stomata | Rain leaks in through tiny holes in the plastic of the bag | The bag sweats in the sun | Air turns into water inside plastic
exercise: What are stomata? | Tiny holes in leaves that let gases in and water vapour out | The green colour inside the leaves that soaks up all of the sunlight | The hairs on a root | The stripes on a stem
exercise: What are a cactus's spines? | Leaves changed into sharp points | Thick hairs to keep it warm at night | Roots growing out into the air | Seeds waiting to fall
exercise: Where does a cactus do its photosynthesis? | In its fat green stem | In its long sharp spines | In its roots deep underground | In its flowers only, by day
exercise: How does a pea plant climb a garden cane? | Its tendrils wrap round anything they touch | It glues itself on with sticky sap from its stem | Its roots climb up the cane | The gardener ties every leaf
exercise: How does a stinging nettle sting? | Hairs with glass-like tips snap off and inject stinging chemicals | It shoots tiny darts at you | Its leaves are covered in a burning acid all the time, top and bottom | It bites with hidden teeth
exercise: What are the layers of an onion? | Swollen leaves storing food | Rings of root grown one each year | Layers of soil it has soaked up | Old skins left from past years
exercise: Why do maple leaves turn red and orange in autumn? | The green goes, so hidden colours show and some new reds are made | The cold frost paints them | The leaves catch fire slowly | Birds bring the colour in their beaks from the berries they eat
exercise: What makes the sensitive plant fold its leaves so fast? | Water rushes out of cells at the base of each leaf | Tiny muscles pull the leaves shut | Nerves send a message to its brain | The wind from your hand blows them shut, however gently you move
exercise: How does a giant water lily leaf, up to 3 metres across, stay afloat? | Strong ribs with air between them | It sits right on the bottom of the pond | It is filled inside with light oil | Fish hold it up from below the water
exercise: What famous building was inspired by the ribs of the giant water lily leaf? | The Crystal Palace in London | The Eiffel Tower in Paris, France | The Leaning Tower of Pisa in Italy | The Great Wall in northern China
test: Why does a dandelion grow back after you pull off its leaves? | The deep taproot is still there with its food store | Dandelions grow from their leaves | The wind blows new leaves on from all the other dandelions nearby | The soil remembers the plant
test: What would happen if you put a white flower in red water? | The petals would slowly turn pinkish-red | The red water would slowly turn clear and white | The flower would turn green | Nothing at all would change
test: Why should you be gentle with the roots when moving a plant to a new pot? | Breaking the tiny root hairs stops it soaking up water | Roots feel pain like fingers | Roots explode when touched | The soil will fall off and the leaves will dry up in a day
test: Why are most leaves flat instead of round like a ball? | Flat leaves catch more light and let gases in and out | Round leaves would roll away | Flat leaves are easier for insects to eat | Leaves are flat only because they are squashed in the bud
test: Why would a cactus open its stomata at night instead of during the day? | It loses much less water in the cool night | Cacti only breathe in the dark, just like bats do | The hot Sun burns the tiny holes shut | It is hiding from bees
test: in my cartoon the plant drinks the rain through its leaves when it pours. that how it works? | Not really, almost all its water goes in through the roots | Yes, leaves are like sponges and soak up every drop that lands | Yes, roots are just for holding on | No, plants don't need water
test: What would happen to a pea plant with nothing to climb? | It would sprawl on the ground and get less light | It would grow a thick woody trunk like a small tree | It would climb its own leaves | It would grow faster than ever
test: Why might many butterflies choose nettles for their caterpillars? | Few animals eat nettles, so the caterpillars are safer there | Nettles taste much sweeter than any of the other leaves in the hedge | The sting makes caterpillars strong | Butterflies cannot see any other plants
test: Is a carrot a root or a stem, and how could you tell? | A root: it has no buds or leaves along it and grows down | A stem: it is orange inside | A leaf: it is fat and stored underground, away from the light | A fruit: it has seeds
test: Why do many trees drop their leaves in winter instead of keeping them? | Thin leaves would be hurt by frost and lose water when the ground freezes | Trees get bored of the same leaves | The leaves get too heavy with snow to keep growing, so they break off the twigs | Birds pull them off for nests
test: My mate says the sensitive plant has muscles like us. right? | No, plants have no muscles; water moving in cells makes it fold | Yes, tiny green muscles | Yes, but only in the leaves and the stalks, not the roots | No, someone blows on it to make it fold
test: Why would a giant water lily leaf need spikes on its underside? | To protect it from fish and other animals that would eat it | To catch flies like a trap | To hold it down to the bottom of the pond in a storm | To help it swim
exam: Identify the principal function of the root hairs of a plant. | The enlargement of the surface which absorbs water and minerals | The anchorage of the plant in the air | The production of seeds beneath the soil | The storage of sugar for winter
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the phenomenon of transpiration? | The loss of water vapour through the stomata, drawing water upward | The absorption of rain directly through the bark | The production of oxygen within the roots | The descent of sap from the leaves into the soil
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the spines of a cactus. | They are modified leaves, and photosynthesis is conducted in the stem | They are modified roots, and photosynthesis is conducted in the spines | They are hairs that conduct water into the plant | They are seeds awaiting dispersal by wind
exam: Ascertain the cause of the autumnal reddening and yellowing of the leaves of deciduous trees. | The breakdown of chlorophyll, revealing other pigments, some red ones newly made | The deposition of colour by frost upon the surface | The combustion of the leaf through the summer heat | The absorption of red light from the setting Sun
